}
\section{Function Provenance and Modifications to Kim \emph{et al.} 2015}{

The original code for this function from Kim _et al. 2015 set up 120 day
growing season vector for \code{RRG}. The paper used an 88 day \code{duration}, this
function has been modified to use an \code{RRG} suitable for 88 days.

The original code for this function also had an incorrect matrix for the
relative rate of physiological growth modifier, \code{RRG} or \code{RRPhysioloGrowth}
in Kim \emph{et al.} 2015. The original
matrix had the following values:
\itemize{
\item Hours: \verb{c(4L, 6L, 8L, 10L, 12L, 14L, 16L, 18L, 20L, 22L, 24L}
\item Modifier: c(0.15, 0.21, 0.11, 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.001, 0.005, 0.005, 0.005,
0.005, 0.005, 0.005, 0.005))
\pkg{epicrop} uses the following values:
\item Hours: \verb{c(4L, 6L, 8L, 10L, 12L, 14L, 16L, 18L, 20L, 22L, 24L}
\item Modifier: c(0.15, 0.21, 0.11, 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.0001,
0.0001, 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.0001)
}
}

\section{Function Provenance and Modifications}{

This function is adapted from Kim \emph{et al.} by Adam H. Sparks, Curtin
University, with assistance from Kwang-Hyung Kim, APEC Climate Center,
12 Centum7-ro, Haeundae-gu, Busan 612-020, South Korea.
Original model development: Serge Savary & Rene Pangga (IRRI).
Original \R implementation by Robert J. Hijmans, Rene Pangga, & Jorrel
Aunario (IRRI).

While the paper by Kim \emph{et al.} 2015 indicates that the leaf
wetness duration changed with time rather than being 0 or 1 as in Savary
\emph{et al.} 2012, the original code available does not implement this.
\pkg{epicrop} offers the option to do this by setting
\code{simple_wetness = FALSE}, but the results may differ from the published
values if this is used.  Further changes were made to the length of the
growing season where the example code ran for 100 days, the paper ran for 77.
This function has been written to match the paper's 77 day growing season
duration and RcA and RRG have been modified accordingly.

If the \code{wth} object provides \emph{LAT} and \emph{LON} columns, these will be included
in the output for mapping purposes. Both values must be present. These
columns are provided by default when using \code{\link[=get_wth]{get_wth()}}.

The optimum temperature for leaf blast as presented in Table 2 of
Savary \emph{et al.} 2012 has a typo. The optimal value should be 20 °C, not
25 °C as shown. The correct value, 20 °C, is used in this implementation.
}

\section{TMIN/TMAX Details}{


If \code{simple_wetness} is set to \code{FALSE}, the function will use the \code{TMIN} and
\code{TMAX} columns in the \code{wth} object to calculate the leaf wetness value for
24 hours of the day using the \code{rhlim} and \code{rainlim} values and then uses
\code{RcW} to calculate a value between 0 and 1 for the whole day.   When
\code{simple_wetness} is set to \code{TRUE}, the function only sets the leaf wetness
to 0 or 1 for the day based on the \code{rhlim} and \code{rainlim} values.
}

\section{LAT/LON Details}{


If the \code{wth} object provides \code{LAT} and \code{LON} columns, these will be included
in the output for mapping purposes.  Both values must be present.  These
columns are provided by default when using \code{\link[=get_wth]{get_wth()}}.
}

\examples{
\dontshow{if (interactive()) withAutoprint(\{ # examplesIf}
# get weather for IRRI Zeigler Experiment Station in wet season 2000
wth <- get_wth(
  lonlat = c(121.25562, 14.6774),
  dates = c("2000-05-01", "2000-09-01")
)

# use 25 May as transplanting date (`emergence`) as in Kim et al. 2015
sb_sim <- modified_kim_sheath_blight(wth, emergence = "2000-05-25")
plot(x = sb_sim$dates, y = sb_sim$intensity, type = "l")
\dontshow{\}) # examplesIf}
